@SherifWaly Want to do this? I would do as follows: 

* class ``AbstractSystemProperty`` implements ``SystemProperty``, which has a String attribute, ``name``.
 In the ``read()`` method you simply have ``System.getProperty(this.name)``
* the classes for each of the 4 interfaces implement the respective interface and extend ``AbstractSystemProperty``

e.g. for ``Region``:

```
/**
 * Region as a system property.
 */
public final class StRegion implements Region extends AbstractSystemProperty {
    public StRegion(final String name) {
        super(name);
    }
}
```

As I explained in the [article](http://www.amihaiemil.com/2017/02/24/each-system-property-a-class.html) these classes cannot really be unit tested, since we end up with the same problem we're trying to avoid :) 

And I would add a puzzle, with ``15min`` estimation, for adding of the ``Fake`` implementations in each of the 4 interfaces.

What do you think? :D
